add(
  "math",
  "linear_programming",
  "Feasible Regions",
  "Reading a Feasible Region",

  `
<h2>Reading a Feasible Region</h2>

<h3>What does a feasible region tell us?</h3>

<p>
Every point in the feasible region represents a combination of values
that obeys all the restrictions.
</p>

<p>
For example, if:
</p>

<p>
x = number of chairs
</p>

<p>
y = number of tables
</p>

<p>
then a point such as (4,6) represents:
</p>

<p>
4 chairs and 6 tables.
</p>

<p>
If that point lies inside the feasible region, the combination obeys
the constraints.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose x represents bags of maize and y represents bags of beans.
</p>

<p>
A point:
</p>

<p>
(5,3)
</p>

<p>
means:
</p>

<p>
5 bags of maize and 3 bags of beans.
</p>

<p>
If (5,3) is inside the feasible region, that combination is allowed.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 2</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
x = hours spent on Activity A
</p>

<p>
y = hours spent on Activity B
</p>

<p>
The point:
</p>

<p>
(4,2)
</p>

<p>
means 4 hours on A and 2 hours on B.
</p>

<p>
If it lies in the feasible region, the allocation is allowed by the
constraints.
</p>

<h3>Worked Example 3</h3>

<p>
Suppose:
</p>

<p>
x = number of shirts
</p>

<p>
y = number of trousers.
</p>

<p>
The point:
</p>

<p>
(8,5)
</p>

<p>
represents producing 8 shirts and 5 trousers.
</p>

<p>
The graph lets us determine whether this combination satisfies the
constraints.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
A point on the graph is not just a coordinate.
In a linear programming problem, its coordinates represent actual
decision-variable values.
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "If x represents shirts and y represents trousers, what does the point (6,4) represent?",
      hint: "Read the x-coordinate first and the y-coordinate second.",
      steps: [
        "The x-coordinate is 6.",
        "x represents shirts.",
        "The y-coordinate is 4.",
        "y represents trousers."
      ],
      ans: "6 shirts and 4 trousers",
      why: "Coordinates give the values of the decision variables."
    },
    {
      q: "If x represents hectares of maize and y represents hectares of beans, what does (3,7) mean?",
      hint: "Match each coordinate to its variable.",
      steps: [
        "x = 3, so maize uses 3 hectares.",
        "y = 7, so beans use 7 hectares."
      ],
      ans: "3 hectares of maize and 7 hectares of beans",
      why: "The coordinates describe the chosen quantities."
    },
    {
      q: "If a point lies inside the feasible region, what does that tell us about the corresponding choice?",
      hint: "Think about what feasible means.",
      steps: [
        "Every point in the feasible region satisfies every constraint.",
        "Therefore the corresponding choice obeys all restrictions."
      ],
      ans: "The choice satisfies all the constraints.",
      why: "The feasible region represents allowed combinations of the decision variables."
    }
  ]
);


