// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 11. SOURCES OF DATA
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Data Collection",
  "Sources of Data",

  `
<h2>Sources of Data</h2>

<p>
Before analysing data, we must first obtain it.
A <b>source of data</b> is where the information comes from.
</p>

<p>
The source may be:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>people,</li>
  <li>observations,</li>
  <li>measurements,</li>
  <li>records,</li>
  <li>reports, or</li>
  <li>other existing information.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example 1: Asking learners</h3>

<p>
A teacher wants to know how learners travel to school.
She asks the learners directly.
</p>

<p>
The learners are the <b>source</b> of the information.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Measuring heights</h3>

<p>
A researcher wants the heights of learners.
She measures each learner.
</p>

<p>
The learners and the measurements obtained from them provide the
source of the data.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Existing records</h3>

<p>
A researcher wants to know the number of learners enrolled in a school
over the last five years.
The researcher uses the school's admission records.
</p>

<p>
The school records are the source of the data.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
A <b>source</b> tells us where the information comes from.
A <b>method</b> tells us how we obtain the information.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>Source:</b> learners
</p>

<p>
<b>Method:</b> questionnaire
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "A teacher asks learners which subjects they like most. What is the source of the data?",
      hint: "Who provides the information?",
      steps: [
        "The teacher asks the learners.",
        "The learners provide the answers.",
        "Therefore, the learners are the source of the data."
      ],
      ans: "The learners",
      why: "The source is the person or record from which the information comes."
    },
    {
      q: "A researcher uses a school's admission records to find the number of learners enrolled. What is the source?",
      hint: "Where did the information come from?",
      steps: [
        "The researcher did not create the original records.",
        "The information comes from the school's admission records.",
        "Therefore, the records are the source."
      ],
      ans: "The school's admission records",
      why: "The records contain the information being used."
    },
    {
      q: "A researcher measures the heights of learners. Are the measuring instruments the main source of the data?",
      hint: "The instrument measures, but whose height is being recorded?",
      steps: [
        "The instrument is used to obtain the measurements.",
        "The measurements describe the learners.",
        "Therefore, the learners are the source, while the instrument is a tool for collecting the data."
      ],
      ans: "No. The learners are the source.",
      why: "The instrument is a collection tool; the source is the group from which the information originates."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 12. METHODS OF COLLECTING DATA
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Data Collection",
  "Methods of Collecting Data",

  `
<h2>Methods of Collecting Data</h2>

<p>
A <b>method of collecting data</b> is the way we obtain information
from its source.
</p>

<p>
Common methods include:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>questionnaires,</li>
  <li>interviews,</li>
  <li>observation,</li>
  <li>measurement, and</li>
  <li>experiments.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example 1: Questionnaire</h3>

<p>
A teacher gives learners a form containing questions about the number
of hours they study each day.
</p>

<p>
The method is a <b>questionnaire</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 2: Interview</h3>

<p>
A researcher asks farmers questions face-to-face and records their
answers.
</p>

<p>
The method is an <b>interview</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 3: Observation</h3>

<p>
A researcher counts the number of vehicles passing a road in one hour
without asking the drivers.
</p>

<p>
The researcher is using <b>observation</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 4: Measurement</h3>

<p>
A teacher uses a ruler to measure the heights of learners.
</p>

<p>
The method is <b>measurement</b>.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Do not confuse the <b>source</b> with the <b>method</b>.
</p>

<p>
The source answers:
<b>"Where does the information come from?"</b>
</p>

<p>
The method answers:
<b>"How is the information obtained?"</b>
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "A researcher gives 100 learners a form containing questions and collects their completed answers. Which method is being used?",
      hint: "Think about a written set of questions.",
      steps: [
        "The learners are given questions to answer.",
        "Their answers are collected using a form.",
        "Therefore, the method is a questionnaire."
      ],
      ans: "Questionnaire",
      why: "A questionnaire collects information through a set of questions."
    },
    {
      q: "A researcher watches vehicles passing a road and records their numbers. Which method is being used?",
      hint: "The researcher is watching and recording.",
      steps: [
        "The researcher does not ask the drivers questions.",
        "The researcher watches what happens.",
        "Therefore, the method is observation."
      ],
      ans: "Observation",
      why: "The information is obtained by watching and recording events."
    },
    {
      q: "A teacher uses a measuring tape to record the heights of learners. Which method is used?",
      hint: "What is the teacher doing to obtain the values?",
      steps: [
        "The teacher uses an instrument.",
        "The teacher obtains numerical values by measuring height.",
        "Therefore, the method is measurement."
      ],
      ans: "Measurement",
      why: "The values are obtained by directly measuring a characteristic."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 13. TALLY MARKS
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Data Collection",
  "Tally Marks",

  `
<h2>Tally Marks</h2>

<p>
A <b>tally mark</b> is a quick way of recording how many times an
observation occurs.
</p>

<p>
We normally record tallies in groups of five.
</p>

<p>
The first four observations are written as:
</p>

<p><b>| | | |</b></p>

<p>
The fifth observation crosses the first four:
</p>

<p><b>||||</b> with the fifth stroke crossing them.
</p>

<p>
This group represents <b>5</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose the number 3 occurs seven times.
</p>

<p>
We record the first five as one group, then two more:
</p>

<p><b>||||/ ||</b></p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Frequency = 7</b></p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
If a value occurs 10 times:
</p>

<p><b>||||/ ||||/</b></p>

<p>
There are two groups of five.
</p>

<p><b>Frequency = 10</b></p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
If a value occurs 13 times:
</p>

<p><b>||||/ ||||/ |||</b></p>

<p>
That is:
</p>

<p>
5 + 5 + 3 = 13
</p>

<h3>Why use tally marks?</h3>

<p>
Tallies make it easier to count repeated observations without losing
track of the total.
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "A value occurs 8 times. How many tally marks should be recorded?",
      hint: "Separate the 8 observations into groups of five.",
      steps: [
        "The first five observations make one group of five.",
        "There are 3 observations remaining.",
        "Therefore, the tally represents 5 + 3 = 8."
      ],
      ans: "8 tally marks",
      why: "Tally marks record each occurrence, with groups of five making counting easier."
    },
    {
      q: "A value has tally marks representing 5 + 5 + 2 observations. What is its frequency?",
      hint: "Add the groups.",
      steps: [
        "The first group represents 5.",
        "The second group represents 5.",
        "The remaining marks represent 2.",
        "5 + 5 + 2 = 12."
      ],
      ans: "12",
      why: "The frequency is the total number represented by all the tally groups."
    },
    {
      q: "A tally shows one group of five and four additional marks. What frequency does it represent?",
      hint: "A complete tally group represents 5.",
      steps: [
        "One complete group = 5.",
        "Four additional marks = 4.",
        "5 + 4 = 9."
      ],
      ans: "9",
      why: "The tally represents nine occurrences."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 14. FREQUENCY
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Data Collection",
  "Frequency",

  `
<h2>Frequency</h2>

<p>
The <b>frequency</b> of a value is the number of times that value occurs
in a data set.
</p>

<p>
In simple terms:
</p>

<p>
<b>Frequency = number of occurrences</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>2, 4, 2, 5, 2, 7, 4</b></p>

<p>
The number 2 appears three times.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Frequency of 2 = 3</b></p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>5, 5, 6, 7, 5, 8</b></p>

<p>
The number 5 occurs three times.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Frequency of 5 = 3</b></p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>1, 3, 3, 4, 3, 6, 7, 3</b></p>

<p>
The number 3 occurs four times.
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>Frequency of 3 = 4</b></p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Do not confuse the <b>value</b> with its <b>frequency</b>.
</p>

<p>
For example, if 4 appears six times:
</p>

<p>
<b>Value = 4</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Frequency = 6</b>
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "Find the frequency of 7 in the data: 3, 7, 5, 7, 2, 7, 4.",
      hint: "Count how many times 7 appears.",
      steps: [
        "Read through the data.",
        "The value 7 appears in the second position.",
        "It appears again in the fourth position.",
        "It appears again in the sixth position.",
        "Therefore, it appears 3 times."
      ],
      ans: "3",
      why: "Frequency is the number of times a value occurs."
    },
    {
      q: "Find the frequency of 4 in: 4, 2, 5, 4, 6, 4, 1, 4.",
      hint: "Count every occurrence of 4.",
      steps: [
        "4 appears first.",
        "4 appears again in the fourth position.",
        "4 appears again in the sixth position.",
        "4 appears again in the eighth position.",
        "Therefore, the frequency is 4."
      ],
      ans: "4",
      why: "The value 4 occurs four times."
    },
    {
      q: "In the data 2, 2, 3, 4, 2, 5, 6, 2, what is the frequency of 2?",
      hint: "Count all the 2s.",
      steps: [
        "There is a 2 in the first position.",
        "There is a 2 in the second position.",
        "There is a 2 in the fifth position.",
        "There is a 2 in the eighth position.",
        "Therefore, the frequency is 4."
      ],
      ans: "4",
      why: "The value 2 occurs four times."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 15. FREQUENCY DISTRIBUTION TABLE
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Data Collection",
  "Frequency Distribution Table",

  `
<h2>Frequency Distribution Table</h2>

<p>
A <b>frequency distribution table</b> organises data by showing each
value together with the number of times it occurs.
</p>

<p>
Instead of repeatedly searching through a long list, we can see the
frequency of each value in one place.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Consider the data:
</p>

<p><b>2, 3, 2, 4, 3, 2, 5, 4, 3, 2</b></p>

<p>
First identify the different values:
</p>

<p><b>2, 3, 4, 5</b></p>

<p>
Then count how often each occurs.
</p>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Value</th>
<th>Frequency</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>4</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>3</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>4</td>
<td>2</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>5</td>
<td>1</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>Check the table</h3>

<p>
The frequencies should account for every observation.
</p>

<p>
4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 10
</p>

<p>
There were 10 observations in the original data.
So the table accounts for all 10 observations.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
A frequency table changes a raw list into an organised summary.
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "For the data 1, 2, 1, 3, 2, 1, identify the frequency of 1.",
      hint: "Count the number of times 1 occurs.",
      steps: [
        "The value 1 occurs first.",
        "It occurs again in the third position.",
        "It occurs again in the sixth position.",
        "Therefore, its frequency is 3."
      ],
      ans: "3",
      why: "The frequency tells us how many observations have the value 1."
    },
    {
      q: "For the data 2, 4, 2, 5, 4, 2, what is the frequency of 4?",
      hint: "Count the 4s.",
      steps: [
        "4 occurs in the second position.",
        "4 occurs again in the fifth position.",
        "Therefore, the frequency of 4 is 2."
      ],
      ans: "2",
      why: "The value 4 appears twice."
    },
    {
      q: "A frequency table has frequencies 3, 5, 2 and 4. How many observations are represented altogether?",
      hint: "Add all frequencies.",
      steps: [
        "Frequency 1 = 3.",
        "Frequency 2 = 5.",
        "Frequency 3 = 2.",
        "Frequency 4 = 4.",
        "3 + 5 + 2 + 4 = 14."
      ],
      ans: "14",
      why: "The sum of all frequencies gives the total number of observations."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 16. CONSTRUCTING AN UNGROUPED FREQUENCY TABLE
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Data Collection",
  "Constructing an Ungrouped Frequency Table",

  `
<h2>Constructing an Ungrouped Frequency Table</h2>

<p>
An <b>ungrouped frequency table</b> lists individual data values and
the frequency of each value.
</p>

<p>
We can construct one using four steps.
</p>

<h3>Step 1: Write the raw data</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>3, 5, 3, 2, 5, 4, 3, 2, 5, 3</b></p>

<h3>Step 2: Identify each different value</h3>

<p>
The different values are:
</p>

<p><b>2, 3, 4, 5</b></p>

<h3>Step 3: Count each value</h3>

<p>
2 occurs 2 times.
</p>

<p>
3 occurs 4 times.
</p>

<p>
4 occurs 1 time.
</p>

<p>
5 occurs 3 times.
</p>

<h3>Step 4: Write the table</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Value</th>
<th>Frequency</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>2</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>4</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>4</td>
<td>1</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>5</td>
<td>3</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>Final check</h3>

<p>
2 + 4 + 1 + 3 = 10
</p>

<p>
The raw data contains 10 observations, so the table is complete.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Do not create a frequency table by guessing frequencies.
Every frequency must come from counting the original data.
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "Construct the frequencies for the data: 1, 2, 1, 3, 2, 1.",
      hint: "List 1, 2 and 3, then count each.",
      steps: [
        "Value 1 occurs 3 times.",
        "Value 2 occurs 2 times.",
        "Value 3 occurs 1 time.",
        "Therefore, the frequencies are 3, 2 and 1 respectively."
      ],
      ans: "1 → 3, 2 → 2, 3 → 1",
      why: "Each frequency is obtained by counting that value in the original data."
    },
    {
      q: "Construct the frequency table for 2, 4, 2, 3, 4, 2, 3, 4.",
      hint: "The different values are 2, 3 and 4.",
      steps: [
        "2 occurs 3 times.",
        "3 occurs 2 times.",
        "4 occurs 3 times.",
        "The frequencies total 3 + 2 + 3 = 8, matching the 8 observations."
      ],
      ans: "2 → 3, 3 → 2, 4 → 3",
      why: "The frequency table summarises how often each distinct value occurs."
    },
    {
      q: "A data set is 5, 6, 5, 7, 6, 5. What is the frequency of each value?",
      hint: "Count each distinct value.",
      steps: [
        "5 occurs 3 times.",
        "6 occurs 2 times.",
        "7 occurs 1 time."
      ],
      ans: "5 → 3, 6 → 2, 7 → 1",
      why: "Each frequency comes directly from counting the original observations."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 17. READING AN UNGROUPED FREQUENCY TABLE
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Data Collection",
  "Reading an Ungrouped Frequency Table",

  `
<h2>Reading an Ungrouped Frequency Table</h2>

<p>
Once a frequency table has been constructed, we must be able to read
the information correctly.
</p>

<p>
Remember:
</p>

<p>
<b>Value</b> = the observation being recorded.
</p>

<p>
<b>Frequency</b> = how many times that observation occurs.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Number of books</th>
<th>Frequency</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>0</td>
<td>2</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1</td>
<td>5</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>7</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>4</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>Reading the table</h3>

<p>
The value 2 has frequency 7.
</p>

<p>
This means <b>7 learners have 2 books</b>.
</p>

<p>
The value 3 has frequency 4.
</p>

<p>
This means <b>4 learners have 3 books</b>.
</p>

<h3>Do not reverse them</h3>

<p>
If:
</p>

<p><b>2 → 7</b></p>

<p>
this does NOT mean seven books occurred twice.
</p>

<p>
It means the value <b>2 occurs 7 times</b>.
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "A frequency table shows that the value 4 has frequency 6. What does this mean?",
      hint: "Frequency tells how many times the value occurs.",
      steps: [
        "The value is 4.",
        "Its frequency is 6.",
        "Therefore, 4 occurs six times."
      ],
      ans: "The value 4 occurs 6 times.",
      why: "Frequency tells the number of observations having that value."
    },
    {
      q: "A table shows that the number of books 2 has frequency 8. How many learners have 2 books?",
      hint: "The frequency tells you the number of learners.",
      steps: [
        "The value 2 represents the number of books.",
        "Its frequency is 8.",
        "Therefore, 8 learners have 2 books."
      ],
      ans: "8 learners",
      why: "The frequency tells how many observations have the value 2."
    },
    {
      q: "A value has frequency 12. Does this mean the value itself is 12?",
      hint: "Separate value from frequency.",
      steps: [
        "The value identifies the observation.",
        "The frequency tells how many times it occurs.",
        "Therefore, a frequency of 12 means the observation occurs 12 times."
      ],
      ans: "No",
      why: "A frequency of 12 does not change the value; it tells how often the value occurs."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 18. TOTAL FREQUENCY
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Data Collection",
  "Total Frequency",

  `
<h2>Total Frequency</h2>

<p>
The <b>total frequency</b> is the total number of observations in a
frequency table.
</p>

<p>
To find it, add all the frequencies.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Value</th>
<th>Frequency</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1</td>
<td>4</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>6</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>5</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p>
Total frequency:
</p>

<p>
4 + 6 + 5 = <b>15</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the table represents 15 observations.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Value</th>
<th>Frequency</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>5</td>
<td>3</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>6</td>
<td>7</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>7</td>
<td>2</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>8</td>
<td>4</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p>
Total frequency:
</p>

<p>
3 + 7 + 2 + 4 = <b>16</b>
</p>

<h3>Why total frequency matters</h3>

<p>
The total frequency provides a useful check.
If the original data contained 16 observations, the frequencies in the
table should add to 16.
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "Find the total frequency: 4, 5, 3 and 8.",
      hint: "Add all the frequencies.",
      steps: [
        "4 + 5 = 9.",
        "9 + 3 = 12.",
        "12 + 8 = 20."
      ],
      ans: "20",
      why: "The total frequency is the sum of all frequencies."
    },
    {
      q: "A frequency table has frequencies 2, 7, 6 and 5. How many observations are represented?",
      hint: "The total number of observations equals the total frequency.",
      steps: [
        "2 + 7 = 9.",
        "9 + 6 = 15.",
        "15 + 5 = 20."
      ],
      ans: "20 observations",
      why: "Adding all frequencies gives the total number of observations."
    },
    {
      q: "A raw data set contains 25 observations. A frequency table made from it has frequencies 5, 8, 4 and 7. Is the table complete?",
      hint: "Compare the total frequency with 25.",
      steps: [
        "5 + 8 + 4 + 7 = 24.",
        "The original data contains 25 observations.",
        "The frequency total is only 24.",
        "Therefore, one observation is missing from the table."
      ],
      ans: "No",
      why: "A complete frequency table should have total frequency equal to the number of original observations."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 19. CHECKING A FREQUENCY TABLE
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Data Collection",
  "Checking a Frequency Table",

  `
<h2>Checking a Frequency Table</h2>

<p>
A frequency table should be checked before it is used for further
calculations.
</p>

<p>
One important check is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Total frequency = total number of observations.</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Suppose the original data contains 12 observations.
A table gives frequencies:
</p>

<p><b>4, 3, 5</b></p>

<p>
Add them:
</p>

<p>
4 + 3 + 5 = 12
</p>

<p>
The table passes the total-frequency check.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
Suppose the original data contains 15 observations.
The table gives:
</p>

<p><b>4, 5, 3</b></p>

<p>
Add:
</p>

<p>
4 + 5 + 3 = 12
</p>

<p>
But the original data contains 15 observations.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the table is incomplete or contains an error.
</p>

<h3>What the check catches</h3>

<p>
If the frequencies do not add to the number of observations, at least
one observation has been missed, counted incorrectly, or placed
incorrectly.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
A correct total does not prove that every individual frequency is
correct, but a wrong total definitely tells us that something is wrong.
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "A data set has 20 observations. A frequency table has frequencies 6, 8 and 6. Does the total-frequency check pass?",
      hint: "Add the three frequencies.",
      steps: [
        "6 + 8 + 6 = 20.",
        "The original data contains 20 observations.",
        "The totals agree."
      ],
      ans: "Yes",
      why: "The total frequency equals the number of original observations."
    },
    {
      q: "A data set contains 30 observations. A table has frequencies 9, 8 and 11. Is the table consistent with the total number of observations?",
      hint: "Add 9, 8 and 11.",
      steps: [
        "9 + 8 = 17.",
        "17 + 11 = 28.",
        "The table represents 28 observations, not 30."
      ],
      ans: "No",
      why: "The frequencies do not add to the original total of 30."
    },
    {
      q: "A frequency table represents 18 observations and its frequencies are 5, 4, 6 and 3. What should you conclude?",
      hint: "Check the sum.",
      steps: [
        "5 + 4 = 9.",
        "9 + 6 = 15.",
        "15 + 3 = 18.",
        "The total agrees with the original number of observations."
      ],
      ans: "The total-frequency check passes.",
      why: "The frequencies account for all 18 observations."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 20. FINDING A MISSING FREQUENCY
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Data Collection",
  "Finding a Missing Frequency",

  `
<h2>Finding a Missing Frequency</h2>

<p>
Sometimes one frequency in a table is unknown.
If the total number of observations is known, we can find the missing
frequency.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Value</th>
<th>Frequency</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1</td>
<td>4</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>7</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>x</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p>
Suppose there are 15 observations altogether.
</p>

<p>
The known frequencies total:
</p>

<p>
4 + 7 = 11
</p>

<p>
So 11 observations have already been accounted for.
</p>

<p>
The remaining observations are:
</p>

<p>
15 - 11 = 4
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>x = 4</b></p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
A table represents 25 observations.
Three frequencies are 6, 8 and x.
</p>

<p>
First add the known frequencies:
</p>

<p>
6 + 8 = 14
</p>

<p>
The missing frequency must account for:
</p>

<p>
25 - 14 = 11
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p><b>x = 11</b></p>

<h3>The rule</h3>

<p>
<b>Missing frequency = total frequency - sum of known frequencies</b>
</p>

<p>
This works because all frequencies together must account for every
observation.
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "A table represents 20 observations. Two frequencies are 6 and 9. Find the missing frequency.",
      hint: "Subtract the known frequencies from the total.",
      steps: [
        "Add the known frequencies: 6 + 9 = 15.",
        "The total number of observations is 20.",
        "Missing frequency = 20 - 15.",
        "Therefore, the missing frequency is 5."
      ],
      ans: "5",
      why: "All frequencies together must equal the total number of observations."
    },
    {
      q: "A frequency table represents 30 observations. The known frequencies are 8, 7 and x. Find x.",
      hint: "First find the total of the known frequencies.",
      steps: [
        "8 + 7 = 15.",
        "The table must contain 30 observations altogether.",
        "x = 30 - 15.",
        "Therefore, x = 15."
      ],
      ans: "15",
      why: "The missing frequency must account for all observations not represented by the known frequencies."
    },
    {
      q: "A table has a total frequency of 40. The known frequencies are 12, 9, 7 and x. Find x.",
      hint: "Add the known frequencies first.",
      steps: [
        "12 + 9 + 7 = 28.",
        "The total frequency is 40.",
        "x = 40 - 28.",
        "Therefore, x = 12."
      ],
      ans: "12",
      why: "The missing frequency is whatever remains after accounting for the known frequencies."
    }
  ]
);
// ============================================================
// STATISTICS — BATCH 3
// GROUPED DATA
// ============================================================


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 21. MEANING OF GROUPED DATA
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Grouped Data",
  "Meaning of Grouped Data",

  `
<h2>Meaning of Grouped Data</h2>

<p>
<b>Grouped data</b> is data that has been organised into groups called
<b>classes</b> instead of listing every individual value separately.
</p>

<p>
Grouping is useful when there are many different observations and a
long raw list would be difficult to read.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose the ages of 30 learners are recorded.
Instead of writing all 30 ages individually, we could group them as:
</p>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Age group</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>10–12</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>13–15</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>16–18</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p>
Each interval contains several possible ages.
</p>

<h3>Why group data?</h3>

<p>
Suppose a survey contains 500 measurements.
Writing every measurement separately can make the data difficult to
read.
</p>

<p>
Grouping allows us to see how many observations fall within different
ranges.
</p>

<h3>Important distinction</h3>

<p>
<b>Ungrouped:</b> individual values are shown separately.
</p>

<p>
<b>Grouped:</b> values are collected into intervals.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Ungrouped:
</p>

<p><b>12, 13, 13, 14, 15, 16, 16, 17...</b></p>

<p>
Grouped:
</p>

<p><b>10–12, 13–15, 16–18...</b></p>

<p>
The second form is grouped data.
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "A teacher records learners' ages using the intervals 10–12, 13–15 and 16–18. Is this grouped data?",
      hint: "Are individual ages being listed separately?",
      steps: [
        "The ages have been placed into intervals.",
        "The intervals represent groups of possible values.",
        "Therefore, the data is grouped data."
      ],
      ans: "Yes",
      why: "Grouped data places observations into classes or intervals."
    },
    {
      q: "Which is grouped data: 12, 13, 14, 15 or 10–12, 13–15?",
      hint: "Look for intervals.",
      steps: [
        "12, 13, 14, 15 are individual observations.",
        "10–12 and 13–15 are intervals.",
        "Therefore, 10–12, 13–15 represents grouped data."
      ],
      ans: "10–12, 13–15",
      why: "Grouped data organises observations into intervals."
    },
    {
      q: "Why might 500 measurements be grouped into intervals?",
      hint: "Think about making a large data set easier to read.",
      steps: [
        "Listing 500 individual measurements can be difficult to read.",
        "Grouping them into intervals summarises the data.",
        "Therefore, grouping makes a large data set easier to organise and interpret."
      ],
      ans: "To organise and summarise a large amount of data.",
      why: "Grouping makes a large data set easier to handle."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 22. CLASS INTERVAL
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Grouped Data",
  "Class Interval",

  `
<h2>Class Interval</h2>

<p>
A <b>class interval</b> is a range of values used to group observations
in a frequency table.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Consider these intervals:
</p>

<p><b>10–14</b></p>
<p><b>15–19</b></p>
<p><b>20–24</b></p>

<p>
Each one is a class interval.
</p>

<h3>Reading 10–14</h3>

<p>
The interval <b>10–14</b> represents values from 10 up to 14.
</p>

<p>
For whole-number data, it contains:
</p>

<p><b>10, 11, 12, 13 and 14.</b></p>

<h3>Reading 15–19</h3>

<p>
The interval <b>15–19</b> contains:
</p>

<p><b>15, 16, 17, 18 and 19.</b></p>

<h3>Why use intervals?</h3>

<p>
Instead of listing every individual value, we can tell the reader the
range into which each observation falls.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
A class interval is not a single value.
</p>

<p>
For example:
</p>

<p>
<b>12</b> is a value.
</p>

<p>
<b>10–14</b> is a class interval.
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "Which of the following is a class interval: 17 or 15–19?",
      hint: "A class interval represents a range.",
      steps: [
        "17 represents one value.",
        "15–19 represents a range of values.",
        "Therefore, 15–19 is the class interval."
      ],
      ans: "15–19",
      why: "A class interval contains a range of values."
    },
    {
      q: "Which whole-number values are included in the class interval 20–24?",
      hint: "Start at 20 and continue to 24.",
      steps: [
        "Start with 20.",
        "Then 21, 22 and 23.",
        "Finally 24.",
        "Therefore, the values are 20, 21, 22, 23 and 24."
      ],
      ans: "20, 21, 22, 23 and 24",
      why: "The interval includes every whole-number value from 20 to 24."
    },
    {
      q: "In a table, the class intervals are 5–9, 10–14 and 15–19. Which interval contains the value 13?",
      hint: "Check where 13 falls.",
      steps: [
        "13 is greater than or equal to 10.",
        "13 is less than or equal to 14.",
        "Therefore, 13 belongs to the interval 10–14."
      ],
      ans: "10–14",
      why: "13 lies within the limits 10 and 14."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 23. CLASS LIMITS
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Grouped Data",
  "Class Limits",

  `
<h2>Class Limits</h2>

<p>
The <b>class limits</b> are the smallest and largest values stated in a
class interval.
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>20–29</b></p>

<p>
The lower class limit is:
</p>

<p><b>20</b></p>

<p>
The upper class limit is:
</p>

<p><b>29</b></p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
For:
</p>

<p><b>40–49</b></p>

<p>
Lower class limit = <b>40</b>
</p>

<p>
Upper class limit = <b>49</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
For:
</p>

<p><b>100–109</b></p>

<p>
Lower class limit = <b>100</b>
</p>

<p>
Upper class limit = <b>109</b>
</p>

<h3>Why are limits important?</h3>

<p>
They tell us exactly which values belong to a class.
</p>

<p>
For example, in the class <b>20–29</b>, the smallest included whole
number is 20 and the largest is 29.
</p>

<h3>Remember</h3>

<p>
<b>Lower limit = first value.</b>
</p>

<p>
<b>Upper limit = last value.</b>
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "Find the lower and upper class limits of 30–39.",
      hint: "Look at the first and last values.",
      steps: [
        "The first value is 30.",
        "The last value is 39.",
        "Therefore, lower limit = 30 and upper limit = 39."
      ],
      ans: "Lower limit = 30; upper limit = 39",
      why: "The class limits are the smallest and largest stated values."
    },
    {
      q: "What is the upper class limit of 50–59?",
      hint: "Look at the last value in the interval.",
      steps: [
        "The interval starts at 50.",
        "It ends at 59.",
        "Therefore, the upper class limit is 59."
      ],
      ans: "59",
      why: "The upper limit is the largest stated value in the class."
    },
    {
      q: "What is the lower class limit of 70–79?",
      hint: "Look at the first value.",
      steps: [
        "The interval begins at 70.",
        "Therefore, the lower class limit is 70."
      ],
      ans: "70",
      why: "The lower limit is the smallest stated value in the class."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 24. CLASS WIDTH
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Grouped Data",
  "Class Width",

  `
<h2>Class Width</h2>

<p>
The <b>class width</b> tells us how wide each class interval is.
</p>

<p>
For intervals written using whole-number class limits, the class width
can be found by:
</p>

<p>
<b>Class width = upper class limit − lower class limit + 1</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Consider:
</p>

<p><b>10–14</b></p>

<p>
Upper limit = 14
</p>

<p>
Lower limit = 10
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
14 − 10 + 1 = 5
</p>

<p>
So the class width is <b>5</b>.
</p>

<p>
The five values are:
</p>

<p><b>10, 11, 12, 13, 14</b></p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
For:
</p>

<p><b>20–29</b></p>

<p>
29 − 20 + 1 = 10
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the class width is <b>10</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
For:
</p>

<p><b>40–44</b></p>

<p>
44 − 40 + 1 = 5
</p>

<p>
Therefore, the class width is <b>5</b>.
</p>

<h3>Why add 1?</h3>

<p>
Because both end values are included.
</p>

<p>
For 10–14, simply doing 14 − 10 gives 4, but the interval contains
five whole numbers.
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "Find the class width of 5–9.",
      hint: "Use upper limit − lower limit + 1.",
      steps: [
        "Upper limit = 9.",
        "Lower limit = 5.",
        "9 − 5 + 1 = 5."
      ],
      ans: "5",
      why: "The interval contains the five values 5, 6, 7, 8 and 9."
    },
    {
      q: "Find the class width of 20–29.",
      hint: "Subtract the lower limit from the upper limit, then add 1.",
      steps: [
        "29 − 20 = 9.",
        "9 + 1 = 10.",
        "Therefore, the class width is 10."
      ],
      ans: "10",
      why: "There are ten whole-number values from 20 through 29."
    },
    {
      q: "A class interval is 50–54. What is its width?",
      hint: "Count the whole-number values or use the formula.",
      steps: [
        "54 − 50 + 1 = 5.",
        "Therefore, the class width is 5."
      ],
      ans: "5",
      why: "The interval contains 50, 51, 52, 53 and 54."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 25. OVERLAPPING CLASS INTERVALS
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Grouped Data",
  "Non-Overlapping Class Intervals",

  `
<h2>Non-Overlapping Class Intervals</h2>

<p>
Class intervals must be arranged so that one observation belongs to
only one class.
</p>

<p>
If two classes overlap, we cannot tell where some observations belong.
</p>

<h3>Incorrect example</h3>

<p>
Suppose we use:
</p>

<p><b>10–20</b></p>
<p><b>20–30</b></p>

<p>
Where should the value 20 go?
</p>

<p>
It could belong to the first class or the second class.
That creates ambiguity.
</p>

<h3>Better arrangement</h3>

<p>
For whole-number data, we could use:
</p>

<p><b>10–19</b></p>
<p><b>20–29</b></p>

<p>
Now every whole-number value has one clear class.
</p>

<h3>Another example</h3>

<p>
These intervals are non-overlapping:
</p>

<p><b>30–39</b></p>
<p><b>40–49</b></p>
<p><b>50–59</b></p>

<p>
The value 40 belongs only to 40–49.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
A well-constructed set of classes should assign each observation to one
and only one class.
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "Do the intervals 10–19 and 20–29 overlap?",
      hint: "Check whether they share any whole-number values.",
      steps: [
        "The first interval ends at 19.",
        "The second begins at 20.",
        "They share no values."
      ],
      ans: "No",
      why: "The intervals are separate, so each whole-number observation has one class."
    },
    {
      q: "Why is 10–20 and 20–30 a poor choice for whole-number classes?",
      hint: "Think about the value 20.",
      steps: [
        "20 is included in 10–20.",
        "20 is also included in 20–30.",
        "Therefore, the value 20 has two possible classes."
      ],
      ans: "The intervals overlap at 20.",
      why: "An observation should not have two possible classes."
    },
    {
      q: "Which set is better for whole-number data: 30–39, 40–49 or 30–40, 40–50?",
      hint: "Look for an overlapping boundary.",
      steps: [
        "30–39 and 40–49 do not share a value.",
        "30–40 and 40–50 both contain 40.",
        "Therefore, 30–39 and 40–49 is the better set."
      ],
      ans: "30–39, 40–49",
      why: "The classes do not overlap."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 26. CLASS BOUNDARIES
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Grouped Data",
  "Class Boundaries",

  `
<h2>Class Boundaries</h2>

<p>
<b>Class boundaries</b> are the values that separate neighbouring
classes when measured data is grouped into continuous intervals.
</p>

<p>
They remove gaps between classes.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose the classes are:
</p>

<p><b>10–19</b></p>
<p><b>20–29</b></p>

<p>
For continuous measurements, there is a boundary between 19 and 20.
The boundary is:
</p>

<p>
<b>19.5</b>
</p>

<p>
Therefore:
</p>

<p>
10–19 has boundaries <b>9.5 and 19.5</b>.
</p>

<p>
20–29 has boundaries <b>19.5 and 29.5</b>.
</p>

<h3>Why 19.5?</h3>

<p>
The values 19 and 20 are one unit apart.
The halfway point is:
</p>

<p>
(19 + 20) ÷ 2 = 39 ÷ 2 = <b>19.5</b>
</p>

<p>
This creates a continuous boundary.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Class limits and class boundaries are not the same thing.
</p>

<p>
For 10–19:
</p>

<p>
<b>Limits:</b> 10 and 19
</p>

<p>
<b>Boundaries:</b> 9.5 and 19.5
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "Find the boundary between the classes 20–29 and 30–39.",
      hint: "Find the midpoint between 29 and 30.",
      steps: [
        "The upper limit of the first class is 29.",
        "The lower limit of the next class is 30.",
        "Find their midpoint: (29 + 30) ÷ 2.",
        "59 ÷ 2 = 29.5."
      ],
      ans: "29.5",
      why: "29.5 is halfway between 29 and 30 and forms the common boundary."
    },
    {
      q: "What are the boundaries of the class 40–49?",
      hint: "Move 0.5 below 40 and 0.5 above 49.",
      steps: [
        "The lower boundary is 40 − 0.5 = 39.5.",
        "The upper boundary is 49 + 0.5 = 49.5."
      ],
      ans: "39.5 and 49.5",
      why: "The boundaries extend halfway to the neighbouring whole-number values."
    },
    {
      q: "What is the difference between the class limit 29 and the boundary 29.5?",
      hint: "One is an included whole-number value; the other separates classes.",
      steps: [
        "29 is the upper class limit of a whole-number class.",
        "29.5 is the boundary between that class and the next class.",
        "Therefore, they have different roles."
      ],
      ans: "29 is a class limit; 29.5 is a class boundary.",
      why: "Limits describe the stated values in a class, while boundaries separate continuous classes."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 27. CONSTRUCTING CLASS INTERVALS
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Grouped Data",
  "Constructing Class Intervals",

  `
<h2>Constructing Class Intervals</h2>

<p>
When grouping data, we need to create intervals that cover all relevant
values without overlapping.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<p>
Suppose the smallest value is 12 and the largest value is 47.
We decide to use a class width of 10.
</p>

<p>
A suitable set of intervals is:
</p>

<p><b>10–19</b></p>
<p><b>20–29</b></p>
<p><b>30–39</b></p>
<p><b>40–49</b></p>

<h3>Why does this work?</h3>

<p>
The smallest value, 12, lies in 10–19.
</p>

<p>
The largest value, 47, lies in 40–49.
</p>

<p>
The classes do not overlap.
</p>

<p>
Each class has width 10.
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
Suppose the data ranges from 23 to 61 and we want class width 10.
</p>

<p>
We can use:
</p>

<p><b>20–29</b></p>
<p><b>30–39</b></p>
<p><b>40–49</b></p>
<p><b>50–59</b></p>
<p><b>60–69</b></p>

<p>
The classes cover the entire range from 23 to 61.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>

<p>
Good class intervals should:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>cover all observations,</li>
  <li>not overlap, and</li>
  <li>follow a consistent width when appropriate.</li>
</ul>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "The data ranges from 14 to 46. Using a class width of 10, which set of intervals can cover all the data?",
      hint: "Start at a convenient value below 14.",
      steps: [
        "Start with 10–19.",
        "Continue with 20–29.",
        "Continue with 30–39.",
        "Continue with 40–49.",
        "The smallest value 14 and largest value 46 are both covered."
      ],
      ans: "10–19, 20–29, 30–39, 40–49",
      why: "These intervals cover the entire data range without overlapping."
    },
    {
      q: "The data ranges from 31 to 68. Give class intervals of width 10 that cover the data.",
      hint: "Start with an interval containing 31.",
      steps: [
        "31 belongs to 30–39.",
        "Continue with 40–49.",
        "Then 50–59.",
        "Then 60–69.",
        "The largest value 68 is included."
      ],
      ans: "30–39, 40–49, 50–59, 60–69",
      why: "The intervals cover every value from 31 through 68."
    },
    {
      q: "Why would 10–19, 20–29 and 30–39 be unsuitable for data containing the value 45?",
      hint: "Check whether 45 belongs to any class.",
      steps: [
        "45 is greater than 39.",
        "The last class ends at 39.",
        "Therefore, 45 is not covered.",
        "Another class must be added."
      ],
      ans: "The intervals do not cover 45.",
      why: "Class intervals must cover every observation in the data set."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 28. CLASS MIDPOINT
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Grouped Data",
  "Class Midpoint",

  `
<h2>Class Midpoint</h2>

<p>
The <b>class midpoint</b> is the value halfway between the lower and
upper class limits of a class.
</p>

<p>
It is also called the <b>class mark</b>.
</p>

<p>
The formula is:
</p>

<p>
<b>Class midpoint = (lower limit + upper limit) ÷ 2</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 1</h3>

<p>
Find the midpoint of 10–19.
</p>

<p>
Midpoint = (10 + 19) ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= 29 ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= <b>14.5</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 2</h3>

<p>
Find the midpoint of 20–29.
</p>

<p>
Midpoint = (20 + 29) ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= 49 ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= <b>24.5</b>
</p>

<h3>Example 3</h3>

<p>
Find the midpoint of 40–49.
</p>

<p>
Midpoint = (40 + 49) ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= 89 ÷ 2
</p>

<p>
= <b>44.5</b>
</p>

<h3>What does the midpoint mean?</h3>

<p>
It is the centre of the class interval.
It gives us one representative value for the class.
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "Find the midpoint of 10–19.",
      hint: "Add the limits and divide by 2.",
      steps: [
        "10 + 19 = 29.",
        "29 ÷ 2 = 14.5."
      ],
      ans: "14.5",
      why: "The midpoint is halfway between the two class limits."
    },
    {
      q: "Find the midpoint of 30–39.",
      hint: "Use (lower limit + upper limit) ÷ 2.",
      steps: [
        "30 + 39 = 69.",
        "69 ÷ 2 = 34.5."
      ],
      ans: "34.5",
      why: "34.5 is exactly halfway between 30 and 39."
    },
    {
      q: "Find the midpoint of 50–59.",
      hint: "Add 50 and 59, then divide by 2.",
      steps: [
        "50 + 59 = 109.",
        "109 ÷ 2 = 54.5."
      ],
      ans: "54.5",
      why: "The midpoint is the centre of the class interval."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 29. CUMULATIVE FREQUENCY
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Grouped Data",
  "Cumulative Frequency",

  `
<h2>Cumulative Frequency</h2>

<p>
<b>Cumulative frequency</b> is the running total of frequencies as we
move through a frequency table.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Value</th>
<th>Frequency</th>
<th>Cumulative Frequency</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1</td>
<td>3</td>
<td>3</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>4</td>
<td>7</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>5</td>
<td>12</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>4</td>
<td>2</td>
<td>14</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>How the values were obtained</h3>

<p>
First cumulative frequency:
</p>

<p>
3
</p>

<p>
Second:
</p>

<p>
3 + 4 = <b>7</b>
</p>

<p>
Third:
</p>

<p>
7 + 5 = <b>12</b>
</p>

<p>
Fourth:
</p>

<p>
12 + 2 = <b>14</b>
</p>

<h3>What does 12 mean?</h3>

<p>
At the value 3, the cumulative frequency is 12.
This means there are <b>12 observations with values up to and including
3</b>.
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Cumulative frequency is a running total.
It never decreases as we move down the table.
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "The frequencies are 3, 5 and 4. Find the cumulative frequencies.",
      hint: "Keep adding each new frequency to the previous total.",
      steps: [
        "First cumulative frequency = 3.",
        "Second = 3 + 5 = 8.",
        "Third = 8 + 4 = 12."
      ],
      ans: "3, 8, 12",
      why: "Each cumulative frequency includes all frequencies up to that point."
    },
    {
      q: "A frequency is 6 and the previous cumulative frequency is 9. What is the new cumulative frequency?",
      hint: "Add the new frequency to the previous total.",
      steps: [
        "Previous cumulative frequency = 9.",
        "New frequency = 6.",
        "9 + 6 = 15."
      ],
      ans: "15",
      why: "Cumulative frequency is obtained by adding the current frequency to the previous running total."
    },
    {
      q: "A table has frequencies 2, 4, 7 and 3. What is the final cumulative frequency?",
      hint: "The final cumulative frequency equals the total frequency.",
      steps: [
        "2 + 4 = 6.",
        "6 + 7 = 13.",
        "13 + 3 = 16."
      ],
      ans: "16",
      why: "The final cumulative frequency represents all observations in the table."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 30. READING CUMULATIVE FREQUENCY
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "math",
  "statistics",
  "Grouped Data",
  "Reading Cumulative Frequency",

  `
<h2>Reading Cumulative Frequency</h2>

<p>
A cumulative frequency tells us how many observations have been
accounted for <b>up to a particular value or class</b>.
</p>

<h3>Example</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Number of books</th>
<th>Frequency</th>
<th>Cumulative Frequency</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>0</td>
<td>3</td>
<td>3</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1</td>
<td>5</td>
<td>8</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>4</td>
<td>12</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>3</td>
<td>15</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>Reading the table</h3>

<p>
At the value 1, the cumulative frequency is 8.
</p>

<p>
This means:
</p>

<p>
<b>8 learners have 1 book or fewer.</b>
</p>

<p>
At the value 2, the cumulative frequency is 12.
</p>

<p>
This means:
</p>

<p>
<b>12 learners have 2 books or fewer.</b>
</p>

<p>
At the value 3, the cumulative frequency is 15.
</p>

<p>
This means:
</p>

<p>
<b>15 learners have 3 books or fewer.</b>
</p>

<h3>Key phrase</h3>

<p>
When you see cumulative frequency, think:
</p>

<p>
<b>"How many observations are there up to this point?"</b>
</p>

<h3>Important</h3>

<p>
Cumulative frequency is not the frequency of the current value alone.
It includes all previous frequencies as well.
</p>
`,

  [
    {
      q: "A table shows that the cumulative frequency at value 4 is 11. What does 11 mean?",
      hint: "Think 'up to and including'.",
      steps: [
        "Cumulative frequency includes all frequencies before this value.",
        "It also includes the frequency at value 4.",
        "Therefore, 11 observations have values up to and including 4."
      ],
      ans: "11 observations have values up to and including 4.",
      why: "Cumulative frequency is the running total up to that value."
    },
    {
      q: "The cumulative frequency at 20 is 18. What does this tell you?",
      hint: "Translate the cumulative frequency into words.",
      steps: [
        "The cumulative frequency is 18.",
        "It counts all observations up to and including 20.",
        "Therefore, 18 observations have values up to and including 20."
      ],
      ans: "18 observations have values up to and including 20.",
      why: "Cumulative frequency represents the number of observations accumulated so far."
    },
    {
      q: "A table has cumulative frequencies 4, 9, 15 and 20. What is the total number of observations?",
      hint: "Look at the final cumulative frequency.",
      steps: [
        "The final cumulative frequency includes every observation.",
        "The final value is 20.",
        "Therefore, there are 20 observations altogether."
      ],
      ans: "20",
      why: "The final cumulative frequency equals the total frequency."
    }
  ]
);
